test-trapprep: CSR writes, bulk register save/restore, and non-commutative
3-operand arithmetic on objtype=riscv64 - the three things flagged as
needed-but-untested before Phase 2 (9/sun20i trap handling) starts
leaning on them.

WHAT IS TESTED
--------------
16 checks, split into three groups:

  - CSR writes (5 checks, csrtest.s): only CSR reads (MOVW CSR(n), Rd)
    were previously proven, by rv_baremetal-boot-plan9-riscv64-diag.
    CSR writes (MOVW Rd, CSR(n)) are a different grammar rule, never
    exercised before now - and Phase 2's first real act is writing
    stvec to install the trap vector, where a silently wrong write
    means traps never reach the handler at all, with nothing to catch
    it. Three roundtrip patterns against sscratch (a CSR with zero
    side effects by spec design - safe to hammer repeatedly): 0,
    all-ones (-1, catches width truncation), and an arbitrary small
    positive value (catches a "reads back the complement" or "always
    echoes the last write regardless of which CSR" class of bug the
    0/-1 pair alone wouldn't distinguish). Plus one lighter roundtrip
    each for stvec and sepc, the two CSRs Phase 2 will actually touch.

  - Register save/restore mechanics (2 checks, regtest.s): fills R5-R31
    (27 GPRs - R0 is hardwired zero, R1/R2/R3 are the link register/
    stack pointer/SB and are needed for this very function to keep
    running, so none of the four are free to hold a test pattern) with
    distinct, sign-extended values, saves all 32 registers into a
    buffer, clobbers R5-R31 with different values, restores from the
    buffer, and saves again into a second buffer. First check: did the
    initial save match the assigned pattern. Second: does the
    restore+resave match the first save exactly (proving the restore
    direction works, not just the store direction). This is a dry run
    of the exact shape 9/sun20i/trap.c's Ureg save/restore will need -
    getting one register's offset wrong among 31 nearly-identical lines
    is exactly the kind of mistake that's easy to write and silent to
    run.

  - Non-commutative 3-operand arithmetic (9 checks, arithtest.s): SUB/
    SLT/SLTU/SLL/SRL/SRA all share optab.c's case-0/OP_R path and the
    identical "oprrr rreg ',' sreg ',' rreg" grammar rule that bug #4
    (PLAN9_RISCV64_LESSONS_LEARNED.md) fixed for the branch family. The
    fix covers these too - confirmed by reading the grammar, not by
    testing, until this suite. Commutative ops (ADD/AND/OR/XOR/MUL)
    would have hidden the same bug the way BEQ/BNE did; these are the
    non-commutative ones where a lingering operand-order mistake would
    actually be visible. sltu_sign specifically reuses the signed/
    unsigned-distinguishing trick from test-branches (1 vs -1) to catch
    a signed/unsigned mix-up, not just a plain operand swap.

WHY THIS CONSTRUCT
------------------
Direct follow-up to bug #4: once the branch-family swap bug was found,
fixed, and confirmed via rv_baremetal-boot-plan9-riscv64-test-branches,
the natural next question was "what else does Phase 2 need that's never
actually been exercised." CSR writes, bulk sequential register stores,
and non-commutative arithmetic sharing the just-fixed grammar rule were
the three concrete answers - all three are either directly on Phase 2's
critical path (stvec, the Ureg save/restore itself) or share code with
something that was JUST proven buggy until recently (the arithmetic
family). Worth confirming all three in isolation before trap.c is
written on top of them, same reasoning as test-branches itself.

One finding from preparing this suite, not tested by it: reading the
real riscv64/include/ureg.h (already existing upstream, not something
this project wrote) showed R6/R7 are the established convention for
up/m respectively - the same register-pinning approach every other
9front port uses. 9/sun20i's Phase 1 dat.h/l.s used a plain global
Mach* m instead, reasoning that single-core doesn't need the
performance shortcut. That reasoning still holds, but it's a real
deviation from this port's own documented convention, worth revisiting
before Phase 2's trap/context-switch code gets written assuming R6/R7
hold up/m the way the rest of this architecture's tooling does.

HOW TO READ THE RESULT
-----------------------
Watch UART0. Each of the 16 lines should read PASS - the two register
checks report which register first mismatched on FAIL, since "some
register somewhere is wrong" isn't actionable on its own. A final line
reads either "ALL TESTS PASSED" or "SOME TESTS FAILED (see above)". LED
heartbeat (PIO bank D, pin 18, same code as test-switch/test-branches):
solid = all passed, fast blink = at least one FAIL. No watchdog feeding,
same one-shot-diagnostic choice as test-branches.

BUILD
-----
mk (produces main.out and main.bin, same convention as every other
rv_baremetal-boot-plan9-riscv64-* folder - main.bin is the flashable
headerless output, built with -s to strip Plan9 symbols).
